<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Guard against a runaway CaptureTests run silently starving (or OOM crashing) a shared gate run.

  This project's tests each open dozens of real Avalonia dialog windows in one process
  (see OwnedDialogLifecycleRegressionTests / DialogTabCycleResidualTests). Investigation on
  2026 08 08, on an otherwise idle machine with no other session competing for resources, confirmed
  this is a REAL leak, not an artifact of a contended shared worktree: a live GC heap dump taken mid
  run (dotnet gcdump, working set about 3 GB at capture time) showed roughly 745,000 live instances
  each of Avalonia.Media.TextFormatting.TextLineImpl / GlyphRun / ShapedTextRun and
  Avalonia.Headless's HeadlessGlyphRunStub / RefCounter / Ref of IGlyphRunImpl, all Avalonia
  internal text shaping and glyph run types, not FreeX application types. dotnet counters confirmed
  the managed Gen2 heap size itself climbs in step with process working set and survives this
  project's forced blocking Gen2 GC after every dialog close, so this is genuinely reachable memory,
  not a compaction/working set high water mark artifact. The leak accumulates per text layout pass
  across the whole test process (every dialog/window open repaints ribbon, grid, and dialog text),
  not per individual dialog or per test method, so it is not fully bounded by splitting one large
  test into smaller ones (see AssignedProductionDialogsBatch1-4 below); those splits remain useful
  for isolating which run is responsible, but the accumulation is process wide. The leak appears to
  live in Avalonia's headless text shaping backend rather than in FreeX's own dialog code; a full fix
  is out of scope for this pass (tracked to report/patch upstream, or to move each test to its own
  process by further splitting into per test .csproj files like the existing Batch2-6 projects).

  Directly measured timing on a clean run: a healthy run completes in 4 to 5 seconds. When the leak
  triggers, working set climbs roughly linearly and the process is forcibly OOM killed by the OS
  (or crashes on its own) within about 30 to 40 seconds of the leak becoming visible. A 5 minute
  timeout would not actually protect against that: the process would already be dead or have
  driven the machine to a few hundred MB of free RAM long before 300 seconds elapsed. 90000 ms
  (90 seconds) gives about 18x headroom over a clean run while still aborting the *test session*
  (not just the OS killing the process) well before an in-flight leak can fully exhaust host memory,
  so a runaway run fails loudly with a clear "test session timeout" outcome instead of silently
  starving or crashing out a shared gate run.
-->
<RunSettings>
  <RunConfiguration>
    <TestSessionTimeout>90000</TestSessionTimeout>
  </RunConfiguration>
</RunSettings>
